<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <!--
    GonogoScansatUplink's OWN contract slice: the wire payload types
    (ScanningVesselEntry, ScanSensorEntry, ScanTrackColor, ScanScienceEntry,
    ScanAnomalyEntry) that used to live in Sitrep.Contract, relocated here per
    the operator mandate that NO uplink-specific wire type may live in core,
    even for an in-monorepo Uplink (see
    local_docs/design/2026-08-10-uplink-types-out-of-core-plan.md). Fourth
    Uplink through the types-out-of-core mechanism, after the MechJeb pilot,
    the Avionics relocation, and the Kerbcast relocation, and the largest so
    far: five types, two [SitrepTopic]-tagged roots (scansat.scanningVessels
    and scansat.science, both isArray), and the first relocation to carry
    NESTED payload types (ScanningVesselEntry.Sensors/TrackColor), so the
    generated shape map moves with the unit map.

    Deliberately its own small project rather than folding into
    GonogoScansatUplink.csproj: same reasoning as MechJeb/Avionics/Kerbcast
    before it, and it matters more here than for any of them. None of these
    five types touches SCANsat or KSP at all, so splitting them out keeps the
    netstandard2.0 codegen leg clear of the UnityEngine/Assembly-CSharp/SCANsat
    HintPath references GonogoScansatUplink.csproj carries; rtcli could not
    load a net48 assembly that needs a live SCANsat install to resolve. This
    project's dual-target boilerplate stays a straight copy of
    Sitrep.Contract.csproj's.

    GPL-3.0-only, matching GonogoScansatUplink.csproj rather than following
    Kerbcast's contract slice to MIT. The Kerbcast slice could go MIT because
    its RUNTIME project is MIT too (it never links kerbcast's own assembly).
    This Uplink's runtime project is GPL by a standing operator decision
    (2026-07-18, see NOTICE-SCANSAT.txt) because it compile-time links
    SCANsat.dll, and carving a slice of that deliverable back out to MIT is a
    licensing call to make deliberately, not as a side effect of a code move.
    Nothing here links SCANsat, so a future MIT re-declaration is available if
    the operator wants it; until then the whole Uplink reads as one licence.
    It stays a leaf either way, so nothing else in the repository inherits the
    obligation.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>netstandard2.0;net48</TargetFrameworks>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>GPL-3.0-only</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <AssemblyName>GonogoScansatUplink.Contract</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>GonogoScansatUplink</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Reinforced.Typings is a compile-time-only codegen dependency: rtcli
       reads the [TsInterface] attributes baked into this assembly's metadata
       to generate this Uplink's own TS SDK slice (see codegen.sh). Must NOT
       flow to net48 (the runtime TFM GonogoScansatUplink.csproj bundles):
       same PrivateAssets/Condition/#if NETSTANDARD2_0 discipline as
       Sitrep.Contract.csproj, for the identical reason (a KSP-loaded net48
       assembly must never try to resolve Reinforced.Typings.dll). -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Reinforced.Typings" Version="1.6.7" Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.0'" PrivateAssets="all" IncludeAssets="compile;build;native;contentfiles;analyzers;buildtransitive" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Sitrep.Contract only, Private="false": SitrepContractAttribute/
       SitrepTopicAttribute/SitrepUnitAttribute/Units are core's cross-package
       surface, provided by the core mod (GonogoCore) at runtime, never copied
       here. Same pattern GonogoScansatUplink.csproj already uses for its own
       Sitrep.Contract reference. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Sitrep.Contract\Sitrep.Contract.csproj">
      <Private>false</Private>
    </ProjectReference>
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
